  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Two scientists dynamited fossils to spite each other. Eighteen seventies America, and they were the founders of dinosaur science.",
        "visual_query": "badlands fossil dig site",
        "visual_fallback": "desert badlands"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Edward Cope and Othniel Marsh started as friends. Then Marsh publicly pointed out that Cope had put a plesiosaur's head on its tail.",
        "visual_query": "victorian scientist portrait engraving",
        "visual_fallback": "old portrait"
      },
      {
        "narration": "After that they raced. Bribing each other's crews, intercepting shipments, and rushing descriptions into print to claim a name first.",
        "visual_query": "fossil bones excavation crates",
        "visual_fallback": "fossil bones"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Crews were told to smash what they could not carry, so the other man's team would find nothing worth digging.",
        "visual_query": "quarry rock rubble excavation",
        "visual_fallback": "rock quarry"
      },
      {
        "narration": "They named a huge share of the dinosaurs a child can recognise today, including Triceratops and Stegosaurus, and both died broke and disgraced.",
        "visual_query": "dinosaur skeleton museum hall",
        "visual_fallback": "museum skeleton"
      }
    ],
